@** The device programs. This part runs on the GPU. OptiX organizes a ray
tracer as a handful of small programs that it calls at well-defined moments:
a \emph{ray generation} program runs once per pixel and shoots the first
rays; when a ray finds its nearest intersection, the \emph{closest-hit}
program computes the color it carries back; when a ray escapes, a
\emph{miss} program does; and for every candidate intersection an optional
\emph{any-hit} program may decide that the hit does not count. Our shading
model follows UsdPreviewSurface: Lambertian diffuse reflection plus GGX
specular reflection, lit by one directional light with hard shadows and by
one glossy reflection bounce, under a simple gradient sky.

The file is compiled to PTX by \texttt{nvcc} and embedded in the executable,
as described in the build-system part.

