@ The launch parameters of the shared-parameters part are declared here as a
[[__constant__]] variable called [[params]]. The name matters: the host tells
OptiX, when it compiles the module, that the launch parameters live in a
variable of that name, and OptiX copies the host's buffer into it before each
launch. The [[extern "C"]] keeps the name from being mangled. As noted in the
build system, \texttt{nvcc} remarks that this \textbf{extern} declaration is
treated as a definition, which is precisely what we want.

The light intensity looks arbitrary, and in a sense it is. The very first
version of the viewer shaded a lit surface with $0.7\,\rho\,(n\cdot l)$, where
$\rho$ is the diffuse color. A Lambertian surface reflects
$\rho/\pi\cdot E\,(n\cdot l)$ under irradiance $E$, so choosing $E=0.7\pi$
keeps that familiar brightness while the shading became physically based.

<<Opening of the device programs file>>=
// OptiX programs: progressive pinhole camera, UsdPreviewSurface-style shading
// (Lambert diffuse + GGX specular) with a directional light, hard shadows,
// one glossy reflection bounce and a gradient sky.
#include <optix.h>

#include "LaunchParams.h"

extern "C" __constant__ LaunchParams params;

constexpr float kPi = 3.14159265358979f;
constexpr float kLightIntensity = 0.7f * kPi;  // keeps the diffuse look of the earlier versions
@ CUDA's vector types come without arithmetic. The CUDA samples ship a header
of operators, but it is not part of the toolkit, so we write the few we need.
Every function is [[__forceinline__]]: they are tiny, and calls in OptiX
programs are costly. [[normalize]] uses the fast reciprocal square root and
refuses to divide by a length below $10^{-10}$, so a degenerate vector
produces a huge but finite result instead of a NaN that would poison the
accumulated image forever. [[mix]] is linear interpolation,
$\mathrm{mix}(a,b,t)=(1-t)\,a+t\,b$.

<<Small vector algebra on the device>>=
static __forceinline__ __device__ float3 operator+(float3 a, float3 b) { return make_float3(a.x + b.x, a.y + b.y, a.z + b.z); }
static __forceinline__ __device__ float3 operator-(float3 a, float3 b) { return make_float3(a.x - b.x, a.y - b.y, a.z - b.z); }
static __forceinline__ __device__ float3 operator*(float3 a, float s) { return make_float3(a.x * s, a.y * s, a.z * s); }
static __forceinline__ __device__ float3 operator*(float3 a, float3 b) { return make_float3(a.x * b.x, a.y * b.y, a.z * b.z); }
static __forceinline__ __device__ float dot(float3 a, float3 b) { return a.x * b.x + a.y * b.y + a.z * b.z; }
static __forceinline__ __device__ float3 cross(float3 a, float3 b)
{
    return make_float3(a.y * b.z - a.z * b.y, a.z * b.x - a.x * b.z, a.x * b.y - a.y * b.x);
}
static __forceinline__ __device__ float3 normalize(float3 a) { return a * rsqrtf(fmaxf(dot(a, a), 1e-20f)); }
static __forceinline__ __device__ float3 mix(float3 a, float3 b, float t) { return a * (1.0f - t) + b * t; }
@* Random numbers. Progressive rendering needs random numbers that differ from
pixel to pixel and from frame to frame, yet cost almost nothing. We use the
usual two-stage recipe of the OptiX samples. First, the pixel index and the
frame number are hashed together with four rounds of TEA, the Tiny Encryption
Algorithm of Wheeler and Needham; four rounds are far fewer than encryption
would require, but enough to make the seeds of neighbouring pixels and
consecutive frames look unrelated.

<<Random numbers on the device>>=
// ---- random numbers ---------------------------------------------------------
<<The TEA hash>>

<<A linear congruential generator>>
@ The constant [[0x9e3779b9]] is $2^{32}/\varphi$, with $\varphi$ the golden
ratio; the other four are TEA's key. All arithmetic is modulo $2^{32}$,
courtesy of unsigned overflow.

<<The TEA hash>>=
static __forceinline__ __device__ unsigned int tea(unsigned int v0, unsigned int v1)
{
    unsigned int s0 = 0;
    for (int n = 0; n < 4; ++n)
    {
        s0 += 0x9e3779b9;
        v0 += ((v1 << 4) + 0xa341316c) ^ (v1 + s0) ^ ((v1 >> 5) + 0xc8013ea4);
        v1 += ((v0 << 4) + 0xad90777d) ^ (v0 + s0) ^ ((v0 >> 5) + 0x7e95761e);
    }
    return v0;
}
@ From the seed, a linear congruential generator with the constants of
\emph{Numerical Recipes} produces the numbers themselves. The low bits of an
LCG are its weakest, but we keep 24 bits and divide by $2^{24}$, which gives
floats uniformly spaced in $[0,1)$ that are exactly representable in single
precision. The seed is passed by reference and advanced in place, so one
seed can serve a whole path, even across calls to [[optixTrace]].

<<A linear congruential generator>>=
static __forceinline__ __device__ float rnd(unsigned int& seed)
{
    seed = 1664525u * seed + 1013904223u;
    return float(seed & 0x00ffffffu) / float(0x01000000u);
}
@* The payload. A ray carries a few 32-bit registers, the \emph{payload},
that the programs it reaches can read and write. Radiance rays use six: the
red, green and blue components of the returned color, stored as the bit
patterns of [[float]]s; the id of the mesh that was hit, for picking; the
recursion depth of the ray, which tells primary rays from reflection rays;
and the random seed, which travels with the ray so that a reflection
continues the same random sequence instead of starting a correlated one.
Shadow rays use a single register that the shadow miss program sets to~1.

<<Payload helpers and the sky>>=
// ---- payload ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Radiance rays: p0..p2 = rgb (float bits), p3 = mesh id, p4 = depth, p5 = rng seed.
// Shadow rays: p0 = 1 when the light is visible.
static __forceinline__ __device__ void setColor(float3 c, unsigned int meshId)
{
    optixSetPayload_0(__float_as_uint(c.x));
    optixSetPayload_1(__float_as_uint(c.y));
    optixSetPayload_2(__float_as_uint(c.z));
    optixSetPayload_3(meshId);
}

<<Recognize shadow rays>>

<<The sky>>
@ Some code, such as texture sampling for the cutout test, runs for both kinds
of rays and must know which one it serves. Shadow rays are the only rays
traced with [[OPTIX_RAY_FLAG_DISABLE_CLOSESTHIT]], so that flag identifies
them. The distinction matters because a shadow ray has only one payload
register: reading register~4 of a shadow ray would be an error.

<<Recognize shadow rays>>=
static __forceinline__ __device__ bool isShadowRay()
{
    return optixGetRayFlags() & OPTIX_RAY_FLAG_DISABLE_CLOSESTHIT;
}
@ The sky is a vertical gradient, from a dark grey below the horizon to a
greyish blue overhead, interpolated by the height $d_y$ of the direction.
It is the background of the image and, through reflections, the only
environment lighting of the scene. It always uses the world's $y$ axis, even
for stages whose up axis is~$z$.

<<The sky>>=
static __forceinline__ __device__ float3 sky(float3 dir)
{
    return mix(make_float3(0.10f, 0.10f, 0.12f), make_float3(0.32f, 0.38f, 0.48f), 0.5f * (dir.y + 1.0f));
}
@* Texture sampling with ray differentials. A texture seen from afar covers
many texels within one pixel; sampling it at a single point then gives the
shimmering moir\'e patterns of aliasing. GPUs avoid this with \emph{mipmaps},
prefiltered copies of the texture at half, quarter, \dots\ resolution, and
pick the level whose texels match the pixel's footprint. A rasterizer knows
the footprint for free, from the differences between neighbouring pixels. A
ray tracer must compute it, and we do so with \emph{ray differentials}: we
ask where the rays through the next pixel to the right and the next pixel
below would meet the plane of the triangle, and how far apart in texture
space those points are from the hit.

<<Texture sampling with ray differentials>>=
// ---- textures -------------------------------------------------------------------
// Samples a texture at the current hit, flipping USD's bottom-left st origin
// to the image's top-left row order. For camera rays the mip level comes from
// ray differentials: the rays through the neighbouring pixels are intersected
// with the triangle's plane, and the offsets are converted to st gradients.
// Shadow and reflection rays sample the finest level.
static __forceinline__ __device__ float4 sampleTexture(int texture, unsigned int prim)
{
    <<Interpolate the texture coordinates at the hit>>

    float2 dx = make_float2(0.0f, 0.0f), dy = make_float2(0.0f, 0.0f);
    if (params.mipmaps && !isShadowRay() && optixGetPayload_4() == 0)
    {
        <<Compute the st gradients of the pixel footprint>>
    }
    return tex2DGrad<float4>(params.textures[texture], s, 1.0f - t, dx, dy);
}
@ OptiX reports a triangle hit with barycentric coordinates $(\beta,\gamma)$:
the hit point is $(1-\beta-\gamma)\,a+\beta\,b+\gamma\,c$, and any quantity
stored at the corners is interpolated with the same weights. The texture
coordinates $(s,t)$ are read from the per-corner array of the triangle.

The last line of <<Texture sampling with ray differentials>> deserves a word.
USD places the origin of texture space at the bottom-left corner of the image,
with $t$ growing upward, while images are stored, and CUDA addresses them, from
the top row down. Sampling at $1-t$ reconciles the two conventions. The
function [[tex2DGrad]] takes the screen-space derivatives of the texture
coordinates; from their lengths the hardware chooses the mipmap level, and
from their ratio and directions the anisotropic filter. With zero derivatives
it samples the finest level, which is what happens for shadow rays,
reflection rays (depth $>0$), and whenever the \emph{Texture mipmaps} switch
is off. The test of [[isShadowRay]] must come before the payload is read,
because shadow rays have no fifth payload register.

<<Interpolate the texture coordinates at the hit>>=
const float2 bary = optixGetTriangleBarycentrics();
const float2* uv = params.triUVs + 3 * prim;
const float w0 = 1.0f - bary.x - bary.y;
const float s = w0 * uv[0].x + bary.x * uv[1].x + bary.y * uv[2].x;
const float t = w0 * uv[0].y + bary.x * uv[1].y + bary.y * uv[2].y;
@ The computation of the gradients has three steps: gather the triangle and
the hit point, build the directions of the neighbouring rays, and turn each
of them into an offset in texture space.

<<Compute the st gradients of the pixel footprint>>=
<<Gather the triangle and the hit point>>

<<Build the rays through the neighbouring pixels>>

<<Turn a neighbouring ray into an st offset>>
dx = gradient(dirX);
dy = gradient(dirY);
@ We need the triangle's corner $a$, its edges $e_1=b-a$ and $e_2=c-a$, the
normal $n=e_1\times e_2$ of its plane (its length is irrelevant here), the
ray origin $o$ and the hit point $p=o+t_{\max}\,d$, where $t_{\max}$ is the
distance along the ray at which the closest hit was found.

<<Gather the triangle and the hit point>>=
const uint3 tri = params.indices[prim];
const float3 a = params.vertices[tri.x];
const float3 e1 = params.vertices[tri.y] - a, e2 = params.vertices[tri.z] - a;
const float3 n = cross(e1, e2);
const float3 o = optixGetWorldRayOrigin();
const float3 p = o + optixGetWorldRayDirection() * optixGetRayTmax();
@ The rays through the neighbouring pixels are built exactly as in the ray
generation program, from normalized device coordinates. One pixel is
$2/\mathit{width}$ wide in those coordinates, and $2/\mathit{height}$ high;
the vertical step is subtracted because the $y$ axis of the image points
down. We use the pixel centre even when the actual sample was jittered: the
footprint changes very little within a pixel. The directions need not be
normalized, since only the point where they meet the plane matters.

<<Build the rays through the neighbouring pixels>>=
const uint3 idx = optixGetLaunchIndex();
const float nx = 2.0f * (idx.x + 0.5f) / params.width - 1.0f;
const float ny = 1.0f - 2.0f * (idx.y + 0.5f) / params.height;
const Camera& cam = params.camera;
const float3 dirX = cam.w + cam.u * (nx + 2.0f / params.width) + cam.v * ny;
const float3 dirY = cam.w + cam.u * nx + cam.v * (ny - 2.0f / params.height);
@ Now the geometry. A ray $o+\tau d'$ meets the plane through $p$ with normal
$n$ where $(o+\tau d'-p)\cdot n=0$, that is, at
$$\tau={(p-o)\cdot n\over d'\cdot n}.$$
The offset $\Delta=o+\tau d'-p$ lies in the plane of the triangle, so it can
be written as $\Delta=\beta\,e_1+\gamma\,e_2$. Taking dot products with $e_1$
and $e_2$ gives two linear equations,
$$\begin{pmatrix}e_1\cdot e_1&e_1\cdot e_2\\ e_1\cdot e_2&e_2\cdot e_2\end{pmatrix}
  \begin{pmatrix}\beta\\ \gamma\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}\Delta\cdot e_1\\ \Delta\cdot e_2\end{pmatrix},$$
which Cramer's rule solves with the determinant
$d_{00}d_{11}-d_{01}^2$, computed once for both rays. Since texture
coordinates vary linearly over the triangle, the offset in texture space is
$\beta\,(uv_1-uv_0)+\gamma\,(uv_2-uv_0)$; its $t$ component changes sign
because we sample at $1-t$. A ray parallel to the plane, or a degenerate
triangle, gives no usable answer, and we fall back to the finest level.

<<Turn a neighbouring ray into an st offset>>=
const float d00 = dot(e1, e1), d01 = dot(e1, e2), d11 = dot(e2, e2);
const float denom = d00 * d11 - d01 * d01;
auto gradient = [&](float3 dir) {
    const float dn = dot(dir, n);
    if (fabsf(dn) < 1e-20f || fabsf(denom) < 1e-30f)
        return make_float2(0.0f, 0.0f);
    const float3 offset = o + dir * (dot(p - o, n) / dn) - p;
    const float d20 = dot(offset, e1), d21 = dot(offset, e2);
    const float b1 = (d11 * d20 - d01 * d21) / denom, b2 = (d00 * d21 - d01 * d20) / denom;
    return make_float2(b1 * (uv[1].x - uv[0].x) + b2 * (uv[2].x - uv[0].x),
                       -(b1 * (uv[1].y - uv[0].y) + b2 * (uv[2].y - uv[0].y)));  // t is flipped
};
@ Scalar inputs. A [[ScalarParam]] is either a constant or one channel of a
texture, remapped by that channel's scale and bias. [[channel]] picks a
component of a [[float4]] by number; the conditional chain compiles to a few
selects, without any indexing into local memory.

<<Reading scalar material inputs>>=
static __forceinline__ __device__ float channel(float4 v, int c)
{
    return c == 0 ? v.x : c == 1 ? v.y : c == 2 ? v.z : v.w;
}

static __forceinline__ __device__ float evalScalar(const ScalarParam& p, unsigned int prim)
{
    if (p.texture < 0)
        return p.value;
    return channel(sampleTexture(p.texture, prim), p.channel) * p.scale + p.bias;
}
@* Normal mapping. A normal map stores, in each texel, a direction expressed
in a frame attached to the surface: the first axis follows increasing $s$ (the
\emph{tangent}), the second follows increasing $t$ (the \emph{bitangent},
pointing to the top of the image), and the third is the surface normal. To
use it we must know that frame in world space.

<<Normal mapping in the tangent frame>>=
// Shading normal from a tangent-space normal map. The tangent frame comes from
// the triangle's positions and st (tangent along +s, bitangent along +t, the
// image's "up"), orthonormalized around the base normal `n`.
static __forceinline__ __device__ float3 normalMapped(const MaterialData& mat, unsigned int prim, float3 n)
{
    <<Build the tangent frame from positions and texture coordinates>>

    <<Decode the normal map and leave tangent space>>
}
@ Over a triangle, position is a linear function of the texture coordinates.
If $T$ and $B$ are the derivatives of position with respect to $s$ and $t$,
the edges satisfy $e_1=\Delta s_1\,T+\Delta t_1\,B$ and
$e_2=\Delta s_2\,T+\Delta t_2\,B$, where the $\Delta$s are the differences of
texture coordinates along the edges. Solving this $2\times2$ system gives
$$T={e_1\,\Delta t_2-e_2\,\Delta t_1\over\delta},\qquad
  B={e_2\,\Delta s_1-e_1\,\Delta s_2\over\delta},\qquad
  \delta=\Delta s_1\,\Delta t_2-\Delta s_2\,\Delta t_1.$$
When $\delta$ vanishes the texture is degenerate on this triangle and we keep
the normal we were given. Otherwise we make the frame orthonormal around the
base normal~$n$ (which may be a smooth, interpolated normal rather than the
flat one): the tangent loses its component along $n$ and is normalized, and
the bitangent becomes $n\times t$. That cross product fixes the handedness,
so we compare it with the true $B$ and flip it when the texture is mirrored on
this triangle; without that test the bumps of a mirrored texture would appear
lit from the wrong side.

<<Build the tangent frame from positions and texture coordinates>>=
const uint3 tri = params.indices[prim];
const float3 a = params.vertices[tri.x];
const float3 e1 = params.vertices[tri.y] - a, e2 = params.vertices[tri.z] - a;
const float2* uv = params.triUVs + 3 * prim;
const float du1 = uv[1].x - uv[0].x, dv1 = uv[1].y - uv[0].y;
const float du2 = uv[2].x - uv[0].x, dv2 = uv[2].y - uv[0].y;
const float det = du1 * dv2 - du2 * dv1;
if (fabsf(det) < 1e-12f)
    return n;  // degenerate st: no usable frame
const float3 tangent = (e1 * dv2 - e2 * dv1) * (1.0f / det);
const float3 bitangent = (e2 * du1 - e1 * du2) * (1.0f / det);
const float3 t = normalize(tangent - n * dot(n, tangent));
float3 b = cross(n, t);
if (dot(b, bitangent) < 0.0f)
    b = b * -1.0f;  // mirrored st
@ The texel is sampled like any other texture, mipmaps included, and decoded
with the scale and bias authored in the file, usually $2x-1$. The decoded
vector is expressed in the frame $(t,b,n)$ and normalized: filtering averages
neighbouring normals, which shortens them.

<<Decode the normal map and leave tangent space>>=
const float4 texel = sampleTexture(mat.normalTexture, prim);
const float3 local = make_float3(texel.x * mat.normalScale.x + mat.normalBias.x,
                                 texel.y * mat.normalScale.y + mat.normalBias.y,
                                 texel.z * mat.normalScale.z + mat.normalBias.z);
return normalize(t * local.x + b * local.y + n * local.z);
@* The GGX microfacet model. A glossy surface is modelled as a myriad of tiny
mirrors, the \emph{microfacets}, whose normals scatter around the surface
normal~$n$. Light from direction $l$ reaches the viewer in direction $v$ only
through facets whose normal is the half vector $h=(v+l)/|v+l|$, and the
specular part of the reflectance (the BRDF) is
$$f_s(l,v)={D(h)\,G(l,v)\,F(v\cdot h)\over4\,(n\cdot l)\,(n\cdot v)}.$$
$D$ is the density of facets oriented along $h$, $G$ the fraction of them
that are neither shadowed nor masked by others, and $F$ the Fresnel
reflectance of one facet. We use the GGX (Trowbridge--Reitz) distribution with
the Smith masking function, as UsdPreviewSurface does. Its width~$\alpha$ is
the square of the authored roughness, which makes the roughness slider
perceptually even. All three functions take $\alpha^2$, called [[a2]].

<<The GGX microfacet model>>=
// ---- GGX microfacet model -----------------------------------------------------
<<The GGX normal distribution>>

<<The Smith masking function>>

<<Schlick's Fresnel approximation>>
@ The GGX distribution is
$$D(h)={\alpha^2\over\pi\,\bigl((n\cdot h)^2(\alpha^2-1)+1\bigr)^2}.$$
For small $\alpha$ it is a sharp peak around $h=n$; for $\alpha=1$ it is
constant, $1/\pi$, over the hemisphere.

<<The GGX normal distribution>>=
static __forceinline__ __device__ float ggxD(float nDotH, float a2)
{
    const float d = nDotH * nDotH * (a2 - 1.0f) + 1.0f;
    return a2 / (kPi * d * d);
}
@ Smith's masking function for GGX, for a direction $x$ making the cosine
$n\cdot x$ with the normal, is
$$G_1(x)={2\,(n\cdot x)\over n\cdot x+\sqrt{\alpha^2+(1-\alpha^2)(n\cdot x)^2}}.$$
It is~1 for directions along the normal and falls to~0 at grazing angles. We
use the separable form $G(l,v)=G_1(l)\,G_1(v)$.

<<The Smith masking function>>=
static __forceinline__ __device__ float smithG1(float nDotX, float a2)
{
    return 2.0f * nDotX / (nDotX + sqrtf(a2 + (1.0f - a2) * nDotX * nDotX));
}
@ Schlick's approximation of the Fresnel equations,
$$F(\theta)=F_0+(1-F_0)(1-\cos\theta)^5,$$
interpolates from the reflectance at normal incidence, $F_0$, to total
reflection at grazing incidence. $F_0$ is a color, because metals tint their
reflections. The cosine is clamped to $[0,1]$ so that rounding errors cannot
push the result outside $[F_0,1]$.

<<Schlick's Fresnel approximation>>=
static __forceinline__ __device__ float3 fresnel(float3 f0, float cosTheta)
{
    const float m = 1.0f - fminf(fmaxf(cosTheta, 0.0f), 1.0f);
    const float m5 = m * m * m * m * m;
    return f0 + (make_float3(1.0f, 1.0f, 1.0f) - f0) * m5;
}
@* Ray generation. OptiX launches one thread per pixel, and each runs the
ray generation program. Its name, like the names of all programs, begins with
a prefix that tells OptiX its role ([[__raygen__]], [[__miss__]],
[[__closesthit__]], [[__anyhit__]]); the rest is the name by which the host
refers to it when it creates the program groups.

<<The ray generation program>>=
// ---- programs -------------------------------------------------------------------
extern "C" __global__ void __raygen__renderFrame()
{
    <<Seed the random generator for this pixel and frame>>

    <<Shoot the camera ray through a jittered point of the pixel>>

    <<Accumulate the sample and write the pixel>>
}
@ Each pixel and each frame get their own seed.

<<Seed the random generator for this pixel and frame>>=
const uint3 idx = optixGetLaunchIndex();
const unsigned int pixel = idx.y * params.width + idx.x;
unsigned int seed = tea(pixel, params.frameIndex);
@ Averaging samples taken at random points of the pixel is what removes the
jagged edges of the image; it is antialiasing for free. The first frame,
however, aims at the exact centre of the pixel: its mesh ids serve for
picking, and its image, shown while the reader drags the camera, should be
stable rather than noisy. The point in the pixel is converted to normalized
device coordinates, with $y$ flipped because image rows go down, and then to a
direction with the camera vectors of the shared parameters.

The ray starts at the eye with no minimum distance and practically no maximum
one. The six payload variables follow the layout of
<<Payload helpers and the sky>>; [[depth]] starts at zero, which marks a primary ray.

<<Shoot the camera ray through a jittered point of the pixel>>=
// frame 0 goes through the pixel center (also used for picking), later frames jitter
const float jx = params.frameIndex == 0 ? 0.5f : rnd(seed);
const float jy = params.frameIndex == 0 ? 0.5f : rnd(seed);
const float2 ndc = make_float2(2.0f * (idx.x + jx) / params.width - 1.0f,
                               1.0f - 2.0f * (idx.y + jy) / params.height);
const Camera& cam = params.camera;
const float3 dir = normalize(cam.w + cam.u * ndc.x + cam.v * ndc.y);

unsigned int r, g, b, meshId, depth = 0;
optixTrace(params.handle, cam.eye, dir, 0.0f, 1e16f, 0.0f, OptixVisibilityMask(255),
           OPTIX_RAY_FLAG_NONE, RAY_TYPE_RADIANCE, RAY_TYPE_COUNT, RAY_TYPE_RADIANCE,
           r, g, b, meshId, depth, seed);
@ The new sample is added to the sum kept for the pixel, together with a count
in the fourth component; on frame~0 the sum is simply replaced, which is how
a reset takes effect. The displayed color is the average, $\bar c=\sum c_i/N$,
whose noise falls as $1/\sqrt N$.

The conversion to 8 bits is a deliberate approximation. Displays expect sRGB,
whose transfer curve is close to a power of $1/2.2$; we use $1/2$, a square
root, which is cheaper and was the look of the viewer from its very first
version. Values above~1 are clamped by [[__saturatef]]. Finally, the mesh id is
stored on frame~0 only, the frame the host reads back for picking.

<<Accumulate the sample and write the pixel>>=
const float4 sample = make_float4(__uint_as_float(r), __uint_as_float(g), __uint_as_float(b), 1.0f);
float4 sum = sample;
if (params.frameIndex > 0)
{
    const float4 prev = params.accumBuffer[pixel];
    sum = make_float4(prev.x + sample.x, prev.y + sample.y, prev.z + sample.z, prev.w + 1.0f);
}
params.accumBuffer[pixel] = sum;
const float inv = 1.0f / sum.w;
// approximate sRGB encoding
params.colorBuffer[pixel] = make_uchar4((unsigned char)(255.0f * __saturatef(sqrtf(sum.x * inv))),
                                        (unsigned char)(255.0f * __saturatef(sqrtf(sum.y * inv))),
                                        (unsigned char)(255.0f * __saturatef(sqrtf(sum.z * inv))), 255);
if (params.frameIndex == 0)
    params.meshIdBuffer[pixel] = meshId;
@ The miss programs. A radiance ray that escapes returns the sky in its
direction and no mesh. A shadow ray that escapes has reached the light; its
caller initializes the payload to~0, so the light counts as blocked unless
this program runs.

<<The miss programs>>=
extern "C" __global__ void __miss__radiance()
{
    setColor(sky(optixGetWorldRayDirection()), kNoMesh);
}

extern "C" __global__ void __miss__shadow()
{
    optixSetPayload_0(1u);  // light is visible
}
@* Alpha cutouts. Leaves, fences and rails are often drawn as simple quads
whose texture is transparent outside the shape. UsdPreviewSurface describes
them with an opacity, usually the alpha channel of the diffuse texture, and an
[[opacityThreshold]]: where the opacity falls below the threshold, the surface
is not there at all. The any-hit program runs for every candidate
intersection, before OptiX decides which is closest, and calls
[[optixIgnoreIntersection]] for the transparent ones, so that the ray goes on
as if the triangle did not exist.

Because the same hit-group record serves both ray types, the test applies to
shadow rays too: the shadow of a fern is the shape of its leaves, not of its
quads. For materials without a cutout the program returns at once; the cost
of calling it at all is the price of not splitting the geometry into opaque
and cutout parts.

<<The any-hit program for alpha cutouts>>=
// Alpha cutouts (UsdPreviewSurface opacity + opacityThreshold), for camera,
// reflection and shadow rays.
extern "C" __global__ void __anyhit__cutout()
{
    const unsigned int prim = optixGetPrimitiveIndex();
    const MaterialData& mat = params.materials[params.triMaterial[prim]];
    if (mat.opacityTexture < 0)
        return;
    const float4 texel = sampleTexture(mat.opacityTexture, prim);
    if (channel(texel, mat.opacityChannel) < mat.opacityThreshold)
        optixIgnoreIntersection();
}
@* Shading. The closest-hit program is the heart of the renderer. It finds
the normals at the hit, evaluates the material, and adds up three
contributions: an ambient fill, the directional light, and a glossy
reflection. It then hands the color, the mesh id and the advanced random
seed back to the ray's caller.

<<The closest-hit program>>=
extern "C" __global__ void __closesthit__radiance()
{
    <<Fetch the hit triangle and its material>>

    <<Compute the geometric and shading normals>>

    <<Evaluate the material at the hit>>

    <<Add the ambient fill>>

    <<Add the directional light>>

    <<Add a glossy reflection>>

    <<Return the color and the seed to the caller>>
}
@ The primitive index identifies the triangle in all the per-triangle arrays.
The payload tells us how deep in the recursion we are and gives us the seed.

<<Fetch the hit triangle and its material>>=
const unsigned int prim = optixGetPrimitiveIndex();
const unsigned int depth = optixGetPayload_4();
unsigned int seed = optixGetPayload_5();
const uint3 tri = params.indices[prim];
const float3 a = params.vertices[tri.x];
const float3 b = params.vertices[tri.y];
const float3 c = params.vertices[tri.z];
const float3 rayDir = optixGetWorldRayDirection();
const unsigned int meshId = params.triMesh[prim];
const MaterialData& mat = params.materials[params.triMaterial[prim]];
@ Normals. We keep two of them. The \emph{geometric} normal [[n]] is the true
normal of the flat triangle; it decides which side of the surface we are on,
where secondary rays start, and whether they would leave the surface. The
\emph{shading} normal [[ns]] is the one used for lighting; it may come from
smooth or authored normals and be perturbed by a normal map, so that a mesh of
flat triangles looks curved and bumpy.

<<Compute the geometric and shading normals>>=
// geometric normal: outward for counter-clockwise triangles (see Scene.cpp)
float3 n = normalize(cross(b - a, c - a));
<<Interpolate the shading normal from the corner normals>>
<<Perturb the shading normal with the normal map>>
<<Turn both normals towards the viewer>>
@ The scene code orders the corners of every triangle counter-clockwise as
seen from outside, so $(b-a)\times(c-a)$ points outward. When the triangle has
shading normals, they are interpolated with the barycentric coordinates and
normalized. Should they disagree with the winding, as happens with meshes
whose authored normals were made for the opposite orientation, the authored
normals win and the geometric normal is turned around.

<<Interpolate the shading normal from the corner normals>>=
// interpolated shading normal (smooth/authored normals); they win if the winding disagrees
float3 ns = n;
const float3* cn = params.triNormals + 3 * prim;
if (dot(cn[0], cn[0]) > 0.0f)
{
    const float2 bary = optixGetTriangleBarycentrics();
    ns = normalize(cn[0] * (1.0f - bary.x - bary.y) + cn[1] * bary.x + cn[2] * bary.y);
    if (dot(ns, n) < 0.0f)
        n = n * -1.0f;
}
@ The normal map, when present and enabled, bends the shading normal; the
tangent frame is built around the smooth normal, not the flat one, so that
the bumps follow the curvature of the surface.

<<Perturb the shading normal with the normal map>>=
if (params.normalMaps && mat.normalTexture >= 0)
    ns = normalMapped(mat, prim, ns);
@ All surfaces are two-sided: a ray that hits the back of a triangle sees it
lit as if it were the front. So when the geometric normal points away from
the viewer, both normals are flipped.

Even then the shading normal may face away from the viewer: an interpolated or
normal-mapped normal can tilt beyond the silhouette, which would give negative
cosines and black, or NaN, pixels. We nudge it towards the view direction
$v=-d$ just enough to make $n_s\cdot v$ at least $10^{-3}$, and keep
$n_s\cdot v$, which several formulas divide by, away from zero.

<<Turn both normals towards the viewer>>=
if (dot(n, rayDir) > 0.0f)
{
    n = n * -1.0f;  // two-sided
    ns = ns * -1.0f;
}
// keep the shading normal facing the viewer
const float3 v = rayDir * -1.0f;
const float facing = dot(ns, v);
if (facing < 1e-3f)
    ns = normalize(ns + v * (1e-3f - facing));
const float nDotV = fmaxf(dot(ns, v), 1e-4f);
@ The material. We need three things from it: the diffuse color, the
roughness, and the specular reflectance at normal incidence, $F_0$.

<<Evaluate the material at the hit>>=
// material
<<Look up the base color>>
<<Derive the roughness and the reflectance at normal incidence>>
<<Split the energy between specular and diffuse reflection>>
@ The base color, or \emph{albedo}, is the texture remapped by its scale and
bias, or the constant color. When the mesh is the current selection, the
albedo is blended 60\% towards orange; since the metallic workflow derives the
reflections of metals from the albedo, a selected metal turns orange too.

<<Look up the base color>>=
float3 albedo = mat.color;
if (mat.texture >= 0)
{
    const float4 texel = sampleTexture(mat.texture, prim);
    albedo = make_float3(texel.x * mat.texScale.x + mat.texBias.x, texel.y * mat.texScale.y + mat.texBias.y,
                         texel.z * mat.texScale.z + mat.texBias.z);
}
if (meshId == params.selectedMesh)
    albedo = mix(albedo, make_float3(1.0f, 0.55f, 0.1f), 0.6f);
@ UsdPreviewSurface has two ways to describe specular reflection. In the
\emph{specular workflow}, $F_0$ is given directly as [[specularColor]],
constant or textured, and the diffuse color is the albedo. In the more common
\emph{metallic workflow}, a dielectric such as plastic reflects
$F_0=\bigl((\eta-1)/(\eta+1)\bigr)^2$, about 4\% for the default index of
refraction $\eta=1.5$, without tint, while a metal reflects with the color of
its albedo and has no diffuse reflection at all. A [[metallic]] value between 0
and~1 blends the two. Roughness and metallic are clamped to $[0,1]$, since a
texture's scale and bias could carry them outside.

<<Derive the roughness and the reflectance at normal incidence>>=
const float roughness = __saturatef(evalScalar(mat.roughness, prim));
float3 f0, diffuse;
if (mat.specularWorkflow)
{
    f0 = mat.specColor;
    if (mat.specTexture >= 0)
    {
        const float4 texel = sampleTexture(mat.specTexture, prim);
        f0 = make_float3(texel.x * mat.specScale.x + mat.specBias.x, texel.y * mat.specScale.y + mat.specBias.y,
                         texel.z * mat.specScale.z + mat.specBias.z);
    }
    diffuse = albedo;
}
else
{
    const float metallic = __saturatef(evalScalar(mat.metallic, prim));
    const float r0 = (mat.ior - 1.0f) / (mat.ior + 1.0f);
    f0 = mix(make_float3(r0 * r0, r0 * r0, r0 * r0), albedo, metallic);
    diffuse = albedo * (1.0f - metallic);
}
@ Light that is reflected specularly cannot also enter the surface and be
reflected diffusely. We account for that, simply, by weighting the diffuse
term with $k_d=1-F(n\cdot v)$, the fraction not reflected at the viewing angle.
This is the reason glossy objects show less of their color at grazing angles.

<<Split the energy between specular and diffuse reflection>>=
const float3 fv = fresnel(f0, nDotV);
const float3 kd = make_float3(1.0f - fv.x, 1.0f - fv.y, 1.0f - fv.z);
@ The viewer does not compute indirect diffuse lighting, light that bounces
off one surface onto another. Without it, the faces turned away from the light
would be black. A constant ambient term of 0.15 and a ``headlight'' term that
grows with $n\cdot v$ keep them readable and give a sense of shape, as in
the earliest versions of the viewer.

<<Add the ambient fill>>=
// ambient + headlight fill on the diffuse part (no indirect diffuse)
float3 color = diffuse * kd * (0.15f + 0.25f * nDotV);
@* The directional light. A directional light, like the sun, lights every
point from the same direction~$l$. The surface receives it only if $l$ is
above both the shading and the geometric horizons: with a bent shading normal
alone, light could leak onto faces that actually look away from it.

<<Add the directional light>>=
// directional light: Lambert + GGX
<<Find the hit point and the offset for secondary rays>>
const float3 l = params.lightDir;
const float nDotL = dot(ns, l);
if (nDotL > 0.0f && dot(n, l) > 0.0f)
{
    <<Trace a shadow ray towards the light>>
    if (visible)
    {
        <<Add the diffuse and specular reflection of the light>>
    }
}
@ Secondary rays start at the hit point, but not exactly there: rounding errors
would let them hit the very triangle they leave, speckling the surface with
false shadows (``shadow acne''). We move the origin a little along the
geometric normal, on the side the ray came from. Floating-point error grows
with distance, so the offset does too, from $10^{-3}$ near the origin by a
thousandth of the distance travelled.

<<Find the hit point and the offset for secondary rays>>=
const float3 hitPos = optixGetWorldRayOrigin() + rayDir * optixGetRayTmax();
const float3 offset = n * (1e-3f * (1.0f + fabsf(optixGetRayTmax()) * 1e-3f));
@ The shadow ray only needs to know whether \emph{anything} blocks the light,
so it stops at the first hit it finds and runs no closest-hit program; if it
escapes, the shadow miss program sets [[visible]]. It uses the radiance
hit-group record, whose any-hit program applies the alpha cutouts, and the
shadow miss program. With shadows switched off, the light is always visible.

<<Trace a shadow ray towards the light>>=
unsigned int visible = 1u;
if (params.shadows)
{
    visible = 0u;
    optixTrace(params.handle, hitPos + offset, l, 0.0f, 1e16f, 0.0f, OptixVisibilityMask(255),
               OPTIX_RAY_FLAG_TERMINATE_ON_FIRST_HIT | OPTIX_RAY_FLAG_DISABLE_CLOSESTHIT,
               RAY_TYPE_RADIANCE, RAY_TYPE_COUNT, RAY_TYPE_SHADOW, visible);
}
@ The reflected light is the BRDF times the irradiance $E\,(n\cdot l)$:
$$L=\Bigl(k_d\,{\rho_d\over\pi}+{D\,G\,F\over4\,(n\cdot l)(n\cdot v)}\Bigr)E\,(n\cdot l)
  =E\Bigl(k_d\,\rho_d\,{n\cdot l\over\pi}+F\,{D\,G\over4\,(n\cdot v)}\Bigr),$$
and the factor $n\cdot l$ cancels in the specular term, which [[spec]] holds.
One detail needs care. A directional light is a point on the sphere of
directions, and the highlight of such a light on a perfect mirror is an
infinitely bright point of zero size: either invisible or a single white
pixel. We therefore never let the GGX width for this light fall below
$\alpha=0.01$, which turns the highlight into a small, finite spot.

<<Add the diffuse and specular reflection of the light>>=
const float3 h = normalize(v + l);
const float aLight = fmaxf(roughness * roughness, 0.01f);  // a delta light needs a minimum width
const float a2 = aLight * aLight;
const float3 f = fresnel(f0, dot(v, h));
const float spec = ggxD(fmaxf(dot(ns, h), 0.0f), a2) * smithG1(nDotV, a2) * smithG1(nDotL, a2) /
                   (4.0f * nDotV);  // BRDF * nDotL
color = color + (diffuse * kd * (nDotL / kPi) + f * spec) * kLightIntensity;
@* Glossy reflections. Light also arrives from every other direction: from
the sky, and from the other objects of the scene. A smooth surface reflects
it like a mirror and a rough one blurs it. Computing that blur exactly would
mean integrating the BRDF against all incoming light; instead each sample of
each pixel traces \emph{one} reflection ray in a random direction, chosen in
proportion to the glossy lobe, and the progressive averaging of the ray
generation program does the integration over the frames.

The choice of the random direction matters a great deal. Our first attempt
drew microfacet normals from $D$ alone; the result was correct on average,
but rough metals glittered with noise even after 256 samples, because the
weight of each sample, $F\,G\,(v\cdot h)/\bigl((n\cdot h)(n\cdot v)\bigr)$,
becomes very large at grazing angles. We now sample the distribution of
\emph{visible} normals, the facets that the viewer can actually see, following
Eric Heitz's ``Sampling the GGX Distribution of Visible Normals'' (2018).

<<Add a glossy reflection>>=
// glossy reflection: one direction per sample, accumulated over frames.
// Visible-normal sampling of GGX (Heitz 2018): the weight is F * G1(r) <= 1,
// which keeps rough and grazing reflections free of fireflies.
{
    <<Express the view direction in a frame around the shading normal>>
    <<Sample a visible normal of the GGX distribution>>
    <<Reflect the view direction about the sampled normal>>
    if (vDotH > 0.0f && nDotR > 0.0f && dot(n, r) > 0.0f)
    {
        <<Weight the reflected light and fetch it>>
    }
}
@ The sampling works in a local frame whose $z$ axis is the shading normal.
Any two unit vectors perpendicular to $n_s$ and to each other will do; we
take the cross product with the $z$ axis of the world, or with the $x$ axis
when $n_s$ is nearly parallel to~$z$. A minimum width $\alpha=10^{-3}$ keeps
the formulas finite for perfect mirrors.

<<Express the view direction in a frame around the shading normal>>=
const float alpha = fmaxf(roughness * roughness, 1e-3f), a2 = alpha * alpha;
const float3 up = fabsf(ns.z) < 0.999f ? make_float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f) : make_float3(1.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f);
const float3 tx = normalize(cross(up, ns)), ty = cross(ns, tx);
const float3 ve = make_float3(dot(v, tx), dot(v, ty), dot(v, ns));  // view in tangent space
@ Heitz's method rests on a geometric observation. Stretch the GGX surface by
$1/\alpha$ horizontally and its microfacets become those of a hemisphere; the
visible facets of a hemisphere are distributed like its projected area as
seen from the viewer, which is easy to sample. So we stretch the view vector
into $v_h$, build an orthonormal basis $(t_1,t_2,v_h)$, pick a uniform point
$(p_1,p_2)$ on the unit disk in polar coordinates, and squeeze its second
coordinate towards the visible half of the disk with the factor
$s=(1+v_{h,z})/2$; the point is then lifted onto the hemisphere and the
resulting normal unstretched by~$\alpha$. The final line takes it back from
the local frame to the world.

<<Sample a visible normal of the GGX distribution>>=
const float3 vh = normalize(make_float3(alpha * ve.x, alpha * ve.y, ve.z));
const float lenSq = vh.x * vh.x + vh.y * vh.y;
const float3 t1 = lenSq > 0.0f ? make_float3(-vh.y, vh.x, 0.0f) * rsqrtf(lenSq) : make_float3(1.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f);
const float3 t2 = cross(vh, t1);
const float rad = sqrtf(rnd(seed)), phi = 2.0f * kPi * rnd(seed);
const float p1 = rad * cosf(phi), s = 0.5f * (1.0f + vh.z);
const float p2 = (1.0f - s) * sqrtf(fmaxf(0.0f, 1.0f - p1 * p1)) + s * rad * sinf(phi);
const float3 nh = t1 * p1 + t2 * p2 + vh * sqrtf(fmaxf(0.0f, 1.0f - p1 * p1 - p2 * p2));
const float3 hl = normalize(make_float3(alpha * nh.x, alpha * nh.y, fmaxf(nh.z, 0.0f)));
const float3 h = tx * hl.x + ty * hl.y + ns * hl.z;
@ The reflection of $v$ about the facet normal $h$ is $r=2\,(v\cdot h)\,h-v$.

<<Reflect the view direction about the sampled normal>>=
const float vDotH = dot(v, h);
const float3 r = h * (2.0f * vDotH) - v;
const float nDotR = dot(ns, r);
@ A sample is used only if the facet faces the viewer and the reflected ray
leaves the surface, above both the shading and the geometric horizons;
otherwise the light it would carry is simply lost, as the masking of real
microfacets loses it.

Then comes the payoff of visible-normal sampling. The probability density of
$r$ is $D_v(h)/\bigl(4\,(v\cdot h)\bigr)$, where
$D_v(h)=G_1(v)\,(v\cdot h)\,D(h)/(n\cdot v)$ is the density of visible normals.
The Monte Carlo weight of the sample, BRDF times cosine over density, is
$${D\,G_1(v)\,G_1(r)\,F\over4\,(n\cdot v)(n\cdot r)}\,(n\cdot r)\cdot
  {4\,(v\cdot h)\,(n\cdot v)\over G_1(v)\,(v\cdot h)\,D}=F\,G_1(r),$$
which never exceeds~1. Both $D$ and $G_1(v)$ have cancelled.

<<Weight the reflected light and fetch it>>=
const float3 weight = fresnel(f0, vDotH) * smithG1(nDotR, a2);
float3 incoming;
if (params.reflections && depth == 0)
{
    <<Trace the reflection ray and clamp fireflies>>
}
else
    incoming = sky(r);
color = color + weight * incoming;
@ Only primary rays spawn a reflection ray; the reflection rays themselves,
at depth~1, take the sky in the reflected direction instead, as does every
hit when the \emph{Reflections} switch is off. This bounds the recursion: a
camera ray, a reflection ray, and the shadow ray traced from the reflection
hit, three levels in all, which is the maximum trace depth the host declares
for the pipeline. The reflection ray carries the seed, and receives it back
advanced.

One more problem remained after visible-normal sampling: rare, very bright
pixels, the ``fireflies''. They appeared when a rough reflection happened to
land on the highlight of the light on another, smooth surface. That highlight
is thousands of times brighter than its surroundings, so one such sample in
256 still shows as a white dot. Any value above~2 is displayed as white
anyway, so we clamp the brightest component of the reflected light to~2,
scaling the whole color to preserve its hue. The image loses nothing visible
and the fireflies disappear.

<<Trace the reflection ray and clamp fireflies>>=
unsigned int rr, rg, rb, rMesh, rDepth = depth + 1;
optixTrace(params.handle, hitPos + offset, r, 0.0f, 1e16f, 0.0f, OptixVisibilityMask(255),
           OPTIX_RAY_FLAG_NONE, RAY_TYPE_RADIANCE, RAY_TYPE_COUNT, RAY_TYPE_RADIANCE,
           rr, rg, rb, rMesh, rDepth, seed);
incoming = make_float3(__uint_as_float(rr), __uint_as_float(rg), __uint_as_float(rb));
// Firefly clamp: a rough reflection that lands on another surface's sharp
// highlight of the (delta) light returns huge, rare values. Anything above
// 2 already displays as white, so clamping there only removes the spikes.
const float peak = fmaxf(incoming.x, fmaxf(incoming.y, incoming.z));
if (peak > 2.0f)
    incoming = incoming * (2.0f / peak);
@ Finally the program writes the advanced seed back into the payload, so that
a reflection's caller continues the random sequence, and returns the color
with the id of the mesh that was hit.

<<Return the color and the seed to the caller>>=
optixSetPayload_5(seed);
setColor(color, meshId);
